<h1>How to Verify File Checksums on Windows, macOS, and Linux</h1>
<p>Verifying a file checksum is one of the simplest ways to confirm that a downloaded or copied file matches an expected value. This is useful before installing software, flashing firmware, opening a large archive, or saving a backup. In July, when many people clean devices, prepare travel laptops, or reinstall systems before holidays, checksum checks can prevent avoidable problems.</p>
<p>The idea is straightforward. A publisher gives you a hash value, often MD5, SHA-256, or SHA-512. You calculate the hash of the file on your own computer. If the calculated value matches the published value exactly, the file you have is the same as the file represented by the published hash. If it does not match, stop and download again from the official source.</p>
<p>On Windows, Microsoft documents the built-in certutil command. The pattern is certutil -hashfile filename SHA256 or certutil -hashfile filename MD5. The official <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil">Microsoft certutil documentation</a> describes the -hashfile option as generating and displaying a cryptographic hash over a file. PowerShell also has Get-FileHash, which is often more comfortable for modern Windows users.</p>
<p>On Linux, GNU systems commonly include md5sum, sha256sum, and related tools through coreutils. The <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html">GNU Coreutils manual</a> documents these checksum utilities. A typical command is sha256sum filename. For MD5, use md5sum filename. The output normally shows the hash followed by the file name.</p>
<p>On macOS, OpenSSL is commonly available, and the openssl dgst command can calculate file digests. The <a href="https://docs.openssl.org/3.2/man1/openssl-dgst/">OpenSSL dgst documentation</a> explains that the command outputs the message digest of supplied files and uses SHA-256 as the default digest in current versions. A typical command is openssl dgst -sha256 filename.</p>
<p>Be careful with file paths that contain spaces. If the file name includes spaces, put the path in quotation marks. Also make sure you are hashing the final file, not a partial browser download, extracted folder, shortcut, or renamed placeholder. Most checksum mismatches come from checking the wrong file, a damaged download, or copying the expected hash incorrectly.</p>
<p>When the publisher provides both MD5 and SHA-256, prefer SHA-256 for current security practice. MD5 can still help detect accidental corruption, as explained in <a href="/md5-checksum-file-integrity.html">How an MD5 Checksum Helps Verify File Integrity</a>, but SHA-256 is the better default for new verification workflows.</p>
<p>For important files, write down which command you used. A small manifest such as SHA256  filename.iso  date can save time later, especially when several people handle the same files. Teams should agree on one default algorithm for new work, preferably SHA-256, and use MD5 only when a legacy vendor or old release page requires it.</p>
<p>If you publish files yourself, include the checksum on the same release page as the download and keep old release checksums available. Users often verify files months later, not only on launch day. Removing old hashes makes support harder and can make archived installers look suspicious even when they are legitimate.</p>
<p>The final habit is documentation. Save the checksum beside important archives, backups, and installation images. A small text file with the file name, date, hash algorithm, and hash value can save time months later. Checksum verification is not exciting, but it is practical, fast, and one of the easiest quality checks a normal user can add to a download or backup routine.</p>
